# ── Secrets & environment ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
# Dated .env snapshots taken before a config change. They carry the same live
# API keys as .env itself (Flare, Shodan, FOFA, SERP, CS, plus AUTH_SECRET and
# WEBUI_SECRET_KEY), so they are exactly as sensitive as the file they back up.
.env.bak*
.env.*.bak
# spotter-auth's SQLite store: operator accounts, bcrypt password hashes, and
# campaign ACL grants. Runtime state bind-mounted into the container, never
# source. The -wal/-shm siblings leak the same rows, so ignore the directory.
deployment/auth-data/

# ── Engagement data (never commit real target data) ───────────────────────────
bhound/
bhound2/
*.zip
# SharpHound drop directory. Keep it (.gitkeep) so the README's documented
# default SHARPHOUND_DROP_DIR=./sharphound-drops resolves on a fresh clone --
# note the live value on this host points elsewhere; see README.
sharphound-drops/*
!sharphound-drops/.gitkeep
# EyeWitness web screenshots written by WF06 ingest (client data) live in this
# directory, but the product README ships a small set of checked-in PNGs. Keep
# the bind-mount target and explicitly allow the public screenshot set to track.
screenshots/*
!screenshots/*.png
!screenshots/.gitkeep
# Staging area for oversize uploads that bypass the webhook (ingest_*_large.py).
# Raw client artefacts: SharpHound archives and .nessus scans. The *.zip rule
# above catches the archives but NOT a bare .nessus, which is the 500 MB case.
ingest-staging/
# Volume snapshots from deployment/migrate-backup.sh. spotter_neo4j_data_prod
# is the whole engagement graph and spotter_pg_data_prod the n8n credential
# store, so these are the single most sensitive artefacts the repo can contain.
spotter-migration/
# Extracted target PII exports
extracted_*.csv
extracted_*.json
extracted_*
# Local caches (CVE db, RAG index)
.spotter-cache/

# ── Python ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.pyo
*.pyd
.Python
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
*.egg
.venv/
venv/
env/

# ── OS / Editor ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# ── VSCode (may contain local MCP tokens or workspace secrets) ────────────────
.vscode/

# ── Claude Code local settings ────────────────────────────────────────────────
.claude/

# ── Docker / runtime artefacts ────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.log
*.pid

# Conference material is maintained separately from the public product repo.
/SPOTTER-conference-deck.pdf

# Brute Ratel TLS material is host-specific and is not consumed by this stack.
/deployment/brc4-tls/

# ── n8n root-level config copy (canonical copy lives in deployment/) ──────────
/n8n-task-runners.json

# ── Adjacent project checkouts ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CloudSchism is a separate tool, not a SPOTTER dependency: scripts/cloudschism_
# parser.py reads its *output artefacts* off disk and never imports the package.
# A copy unpacked here once before; this keeps it from being committed if it
# happens again. Clone it somewhere outside the repo instead.
CloudSchism-main/

# ── Engagement artefacts dropped OUTSIDE their directory ─────────────────────
# The rules above scope client data by directory (ingest-staging/, screenshots/,
# sharphound-drops/). These catch the same artefacts anywhere in the tree,
# because the way one actually gets committed is being unpacked "just to look at
# it" somewhere else. Raw scanner output -- the .nessus case the ingest-staging
# comment above flags as uncovered by *.zip:
*.nessus
*.nessus.gz
# Archive formats SharpHound, EyeWitness and evidence bundles arrive in. *.zip
# above is the common case; these are the rest.
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.7z
*.rar
# BloodHound/SharpHound JSON: AD data in the clear -- every user, group, session
# and ACL. Scoped to the collector's stable suffixes rather than a blanket
# *.json ON PURPOSE, because n8n-workflows/*.json and
# deployment/n8n-task-runners.json are source and must stay tracked.
*_computers.json
*_users.json
*_groups.json
*_domains.json
*_gpos.json
*_ous.json
*_containers.json
*_certtemplates.json
*_certauthorities.json
*_sessions.json
# Packet and web captures taken during an engagement.
*.pcap
*.pcapng
*.har

# ── Credential stores and key material ───────────────────────────────────────
# deployment/auth-data/ is ignored as a directory above; these catch a SQLite
# store anywhere else -- Open WebUI's webui.db (the bcrypt admin row), n8n's
# database.sqlite (the credential store), a copied cve_cache.db. The -wal/-shm
# siblings carry the same rows, and deleted rows survive in freed pages until a
# VACUUM, so the sidecars matter as much as the file itself.
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.sqlite-wal
*.sqlite-shm
# Tunnel key material. The operator uploads engagement keys through the
# Infrastructure tab and they land in tunnel-keys/, which is bind-mounted into
# ssh-tunnel-api. Ignored by LOCATION, because the name-based rules below cannot
# catch a key called something like "extravm" -- no extension, no convention.
# scripts/check_workflow_regressions.py has the content check that does.
/tunnel-keys/

# Persistent state under the folder layout: the Neo4j graph, Postgres, the n8n
# database (every workflow AND credential), Open WebUI's webui.db, the Redis
# broker and Tor's guard set. Ignored by LOCATION, like /tunnel-keys/ above --
# these are binary database files with no extension convention to match on, and
# they are the single most sensitive thing the checkout can hold once a host is
# on that layout. deployment/setup-secrets.sh creates the tree; on a host still
# using named volumes it does not exist at all.
/data/

# Vendored upstream checkouts. scripts/bootstrap.sh clones Flowsint to
# vendor/flowsint at the commit pinned in deployment/flowsint.lock and applies
# deployment/flowsint-patches/. It is a separate repository with its own
# history; tracking it here would nest one git repo inside another.
/vendor/

# Private keys. Deliberately NOT *.crt or *.pub -- those are public halves.
# These rules are name-based and therefore INCOMPLETE on their own; see the
# content check named above.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.ppk
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
id_ecdsa*
*.kdbx

# ── Editor / tooling local state ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# .vscode/ and .claude/ are above. JetBrains keeps deployment credentials and
# SSH targets in its workspace files.
.idea/
*.iml
.direnv/
nohup.out
core.*

# ── Compiled BOF objects ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# bof/shareacl/shareacl.x{64,86}.o were tracked once. They are build output, the
# Makefile reproduces them from shareacl.c with mingw-w64, and a committed COFF
# object in a public offensive-tooling repo is what AV and repo scanners flag.
# NOTE the consequence: shareacl.cna resolves the object at RUNTIME via
# script_resource(), so a fresh clone must `make` in bof/shareacl/ before the
# Aggressor script will load.
*.o
*.obj

# ── Secret tiers (SOPS + age) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Recipient lists and ciphertext are install-local. A public clone must generate
# its own age identity and .sops.yaml; never force-add a tier to the repository.
secrets/
.sops.yaml

# The age identity lives outside the repo ($HOME/.config/sops/age/keys.txt), but
# never let a copy in.
*.agekey
keys.txt
